(2. Battery Options and Specifications) Tj
T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(Three battery technologies were compared before purchase. Table 1 lists the example) Tj
T*
(specifications supplied for each option. All three options were sized for 1,000 kWh of) Tj
T*
(usable capacity.) Tj
T*
(Table 1. Example battery specifications \(synthetic test data\). Capacity is usable) Tj
T*
(capacity, efficiency is round-trip efficiency, and price is in USD per kWh of capacity.) Tj
T*
/F3 9 Tf
(Option  Chemistry                Capacity   Efficiency  Cycle life  Price) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
/F3 9 Tf
(A       Lithium iron phosphate   1,000 kWh  92%         6,000       USD 420) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
/F3 9 Tf
(B       Nickel manganese cobalt  1,000 kWh  94%         3,500       USD 390) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
/F3 9 Tf
(C       Vanadium redox flow      1,000 kWh  75%         15,000      USD 610) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
() Tj
T*
(Option A, lithium iron phosphate \(LFP\), was selected. It was not the cheapest option,) Tj
T*
(but it combined a cycle life of 6,000 cycles with a lower fire risk than Option B.) Tj
T*
(Option B, nickel manganese cobalt \(NMC\), had the highest efficiency and the lowest) Tj
T*
(price, but its cycle life of 3,500 cycles was much shorter and it was rejected mainly) Tj
T*
(for safety reasons \(see Section 4\). Option C, a vanadium redox flow battery, had the) Tj
T*
(longest cycle life, but its 75% round-trip efficiency would have wasted too much of the) Tj
T*
(stored solar energy.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
